 "final_text": "I have successfully implemented the Longstaff-Schwartz pricer in C++20 as requested. Here's a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n**File**: `src/lsm.cpp` \n**Header**: `include/lsm.hpp`\n\n### Key Features Implemented:\n1. **Longstaff-Schwartz Algorithm**: Full implementation of the American put option pricing using the Longstaff-Schwartz least-squares Monte Carlo method\n2. **Polynomial Basis**: Uses degree 3 polynomial basis functions (1, x, x\u00b2, x\u00b3) for regression\n3. **Performance**: Optimized to handle 100,000 paths and 50 steps in under 3 seconds with -O3 optimization\n4. **Accuracy**: Works correctly for any spot scale (S0 = 40 or S0 = 40000)\n5. **Correctness**: Ensures price is never below immediate exercise value at t=0\n6. **Standard Error**: Returns the standard error of the computed price\n\n### Technical Approach:\n- **Path Generation**: Uses analytical solution for geometric Brownian motion\n- **Regression**: Implements least-squares regression using normal equations with Gaussian elimination\n- **Backward Induction**: Works backwards from maturity to t=0, making exercise decisions based on continuation value vs immediate exercise\n- **Optimization**: Uses efficient matrix operations and avoids unnecessary copies\n\n### Verification:\n- Tested with various spot prices (40 and 40000)\n- Verified that prices never fall below intrinsic values\n- Performance tested and confirmed under 3 seconds for 100,000 paths and 50 steps\n- Standard error calculation included as required\n\nThe implementation is production-ready and meets all specified requirements.",
